@page "/learn/ssr-client-validation"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Client-side validation · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Client-side validation</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Someone types a bad email into a static form. Why does it take a trip to the server to say so?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Forms · Static SSR · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> a static SSR form checks its rules in the browser, before it posts, and you
        don't change any code. Static forms arrived in .NET 8, and up to .NET 10 they could only be checked on the
        server, after a post.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>New supplier</i> form. It's static SSR: no circuit, no WebAssembly. <b>Company</b> is
        <code>[Required]</code>, and <b>Email</b> is <code>[Required, EmailAddress]</code>. Under its heading, the form
        counts the posts that reach the server.
    </p>
    <p>
        This first form behaves the way every .NET 10 static form did. It runs on .NET 11, with the new browser check
        switched off (Fig. 5 shows the switch). A real .NET 10 build renders the same bare inputs.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="A static form, checked on the server" Url="demo/client-validation/suppliers/new/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: checked on the server" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Type <code>sales@@</code> into <b>Email</b> and press Tab: no message appears. Leave <b>Company</b> empty
            and press <b>Save supplier</b>. <b>Posts to the server</b> goes from 0 to 1, and both errors arrive with
            the page the server sends back.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>The page carries no rules</h2>
    <p>Here's the email input the server sends, as a .NET 10 build renders it:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The rendered input</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;input id="email" name="Input.Email" class="valid" /&gt;
<span class="err">no required, no data-val-*, no rules anywhere in the page</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        (<code>class="valid"</code> isn't a rule. It's the CSS class Blazor gives any field with no errors yet.)
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>&lt;DataAnnotationsValidator /&gt;</code> runs on the server, when the form posts. The HTML it sends
        says nothing about the rules, so the browser has nothing to check. The user hears nothing as they leave a
        field, only after they press Save. Then every mistake is a round trip: a request, the rules, a render of the
        whole page. <code>Enhance</code> makes that cheaper to look at, not cheaper to run, and on a public form or a
        slow phone connection the wait and the load add up.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>The validator you already have</h2>
    <p>There's no code to add. The form is the same code you'd write on .NET 10; what changes is what it renders:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix: no new code, on .NET 11</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="dim">&lt;!-- you write: unchanged from .NET 10 --&gt;</span>
&lt;EditForm Model="Input" FormName="new-supplier" OnValidSubmit="SaveAsync" Enhance&gt;
    &lt;DataAnnotationsValidator /&gt;
    ...
&lt;/EditForm&gt;

<span class="dim">&lt;!-- .NET 11 renders, for you, at the end of the form --&gt;</span>
<span class="ok">&lt;blazor-client-validation-data data-rules="…"&gt;</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        In .NET 11, <code>DataAnnotationsValidator</code> on a static form also writes the model's rules into the page.
        <code>blazor.web.js</code>, the script every Blazor Web App already loads, reads them. It checks each field as
        you leave it, and stops a submit that has errors. Here's the same form, with .NET 11's default.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same form, checked in the browser" Url="demo/client-validation/suppliers/new"
                Label=".NET 11: checked in the browser">
        <Caption>
            Type <code>sales@@</code> into <b>Email</b> and press Tab: "The Email field is not a valid e-mail address."
            appears at once. Press <b>Save supplier</b> with <b>Company</b> empty: both fields are flagged, and
            <b>Posts to the server</b> stays at 0. Fix both and save: one post, the one that adds the supplier.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>
        The two demos share one model, and their pages differ by one parameter. In Fig. 3 and Fig. 4 the highlighted
        lines are code you already have, unchanged from .NET 10: they're highlighted because they're where the new
        behaviour comes from. The only new API is in Fig. 5, and it's the opt-out.
    </p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ClientValidation/NewSupplierForm.cs" Feature="ssr-client-validation">
        <Caption>
            The model both figures post. The attributes are the rules, for the browser and the server alike.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ClientValidation/NewSupplier.razor" Feature="ssr-client-validation">
        <Caption>
            The page in Fig. 2: a plain static form. The highlighted validator line is unchanged; on .NET 11 it also
            writes the rules into the page.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/ClientValidation/NewSupplierNet10.razor" Feature="ssr-client-validation">
        <Caption>
            The page in Fig. 1: the same form, opted out with <code>DisableClientValidation="true"</code>, new in .NET
            11, to bring back .NET 10's behaviour. <code>Enhance</code> and the hidden <b>Posts</b> field are on both pages and play no
            part in it.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Pick a cost</h2>
    <p>For feedback before a post, a .NET 10 app had to pick one of four workarounds:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Four ways to check a static form in .NET 10</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;DataAnnotationsValidator /&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">let the server catch it, as Fig. 1 does</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ every mistake costs a post and a re-render</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip"><span class="err">@@rendermode InteractiveServer</span></pre>
                <span class="why">make the form interactive</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ every visitor holds a circuit open, just to hear "this field is required"</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;InputText ... <span class="err">required type="email"</span> /&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">add HTML5 attributes to the inputs by hand</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ every rule written twice, only the rules HTML has a word for, and the browser's wording instead of yours</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;script src="jquery.validate.unobtrusive.js"&gt;
&lt;InputText ... <span class="err">data-val-email="Email isn't valid."</span> /&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">add jQuery Unobtrusive Validation</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ three more libraries, and every rule written twice: in C# and in the markup</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>Rules in the page, checked by blazor.web.js</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 every static form with a <code>DataAnnotationsValidator</code> gets browser rules, written from the
        same attributes, with the same messages. It's on by default. To keep one form server-only, add
        <code>DisableClientValidation="true"</code> to its validator, as Fig. 5 does.
        <code>RazorComponentsServiceOptions.DisableClientValidation</code> turns it off for the whole app.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>The server still checks every post.</b> The browser check is a convenience. With JavaScript off, or a button marked <code>formnovalidate</code>, the post goes through and the server catches it. Async attributes, like "is this code taken?", only run there.</li>
        <li><b>Inputs inside your own wrapper components get no browser check at all.</b> If every input sits in a component like <code>&lt;FormField&gt;</code>, as in many line-of-business apps, none of them is checked in the browser. The form writes its rules into the page when it first renders, and the inputs inside a wrapper render after that, so they're left out. The server still checks them on every post.</li>
        <li><b>A blank optional email passes in the browser but fails on the server.</b> That server failure isn't new: a static form posts an empty field as <code>""</code>, and <code>[EmailAddress]</code>, <code>[Phone]</code> and <code>[Url]</code> have always rejected <code>""</code>. What's new is the browser letting it through first. Turn <code>""</code> into <code>null</code> in the property's setter.</li>
        <li><b>The browser shows the same messages as the server, translated ones included.</b> The server writes each message into the page, so the localized messages from <a href="learn/validation-localization">Translated validation messages</a> show in the browser too.</li>
        <li><b>Your own attributes can get browser rules too.</b> Implement <code>IClientValidationRuleProvider</code> on the attribute, then register a matching JS function with <code>Blazor.formValidation.addValidator</code>.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
